 We state the needed conditions on a  $(x_i)$  tuple.  Thus condition (i) would be given to all  $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ .  The first part of our solution will be to admit condition (iii), after which we will argue that we can attain the RHS of condition (iii) with ample ease by choosing suitably.

First we would like to see what similarity could relate among the  $x_i$ 's.  Let's take a look at condition (iii).  We notice by squaring the respective numbers, it would give us \[
(\sqrt{1 - x_1^2})^2 + (\sqrt{1 - x_2^2}).^2 + \dots + (\sqrt{1 - x_n^2})^2 = 1\text{.}
\] Certainly this would enforce \[
0 \le (1 - x_1^2) + (1 - x_2^2) + \dots + (1 - x_n^2) \le n\text{.}
\] Thus given the constraints on  $x_i$ 's (i.e.  $-1 \le x_i \le 1$ ), \[
n - (x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \dots + x_n^2) \le 1\text{.}
\] Conditions (i) and (ii) give us \[
x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n = 0 \implies (-1)^2 + (-1)^2 + \dots + (-1)^2 = n \implies n = 0\text{, a contradiction.}
\] Thus we must drop our assumption of condition (iii) and so back to condition (ii) we get we have to hand.  Now we just have to handle the given constraints.

To accomplish this, let us use some representative arguments.  As always, we must take care by checking for the 'boundary cases' of inequalities as our system may hand us with losing some inequalities to be satisfied due to some boundary cases.  Notice that when  $x_i = -1$ ,  $x_i^2$  plus up to  $x_i$  is always one.  This in particular implies that if  $x_i^2 \ne -1$  then \[
\sqrt{1 - (x_i)^2} < 1\text{.}
\] Thus, for the sake of dissection, let's assume the  $x_i$  's are all between  $0$  and  $1$  (both left and right closed) so that it would be safe to have the  $\sqrt{1 - (x_i)^2}$  's all staying strictly inside the interval of the  $1 - x_i^2$  's.

We want to try and dissect our problem by squaring condition (ii)  $n - 1$  times.  We do this to eliminate some troublesome roots that we would have had if we stayed with condition (iii), and instead we would have to illuminate that this is our culprit given the constraints on  $x_i$  's.  This approach is going to hurt our eyes a little bit, so we're going to do it one last time on the second go around here.  We start with the base of squaring, giving us \[
\binom{n}{2}(\sqrt{1 - x_1^2})^2 + (\sqrt{1 - x_1^2}\sqrt{1 - x_2^2})^2\text{,}
\] which is simply: \[
\binom{n}{2} (1 - x_1^2) + (1 - x_1^2)(1 - x_2^2)\text{.}
\]By the process of expanding new expressions on the RHS of this identity (unpressurized by a corresponding expansion going across the first equal sign, which must match RHS equations by definition) as well as distribution, we conclude that  \[
\sqrt{1 - x_1^2} + \sqrt{1 - x_2^2} + \dots + \sqrt{1 - x_n^2} \\
= (n - 1) + \binom{n}{2} (x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n) -  \binom{n}{4} (x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n)^2
\]because given our base they both equal  $1$ .  This implies that \[
x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n = - (n - 1) - \binom{n}{2} + \binom{n}{4} \left( {\frac{n}{2}} \right)^2\text{.}
\]Thanks to condition (ii), this means \[
\frac{ n ^ 2 } { 4 } - \binom{n}{2} = - \left( n - 1 \right) \quad \implies \quad \binom{n}{4} = 1 - \frac{ n - 1 } { 4 }\text{.}
\]The last part of our equality gave us \[
\binom{n}{4} = \frac{ [n(n - 1)(n - 2)(n - 3) / 24 ] } { [n(n - 1)(n - 2)/6] } = \frac{n-3}{4}\text{.}
\]But we know this means $ \dfrac{n-3}{4} -1 + \dfrac{n - 1}{4} = 1$, which finally inside a LCM constrains us to $n=4$ and so therefore the answer is $\boxed{4}$.

